# Pantheon's plan, in John Carmack's .plan format (docs/PLAN.md): one item
# per line, under the day it was written. An unmarked line is open; a bid
# marks its item "+ " when done (or "* " the day it was written), "- " if
# dropped. Marked lines are pruned once they've landed, so the file holds
# what's left: `git log -p .plan` has the rest. The order is the priority,
# a guide: the leader may take another item when it's the better next step.
# An item is a pointer: its details are in the files it names.

2026-11-10
shell into Soma, step 4: remaining perform cases into Soma: broadcast, height, behind, status, merge (with edit_once), submit, llm (prompt assembly with replace_all and plan_open leaving the helper record), and check. Timer, log, reveal (including hash iteration and fault injection), and decided persistence now run in Soma over small bindings, at startup/replay as well as in the loop; pure plans have concrete Examples. Process, git and socket calls stay bindings. Floor: node behavior and speed unchanged, build/proofs/edits/e2e pass, including silent timing. Finishers mark this line and add step 5 (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Next" 4 and "Two directions").
M-2: the market for n agents: a merge needs n - f approvals (f = (n-1)/3), f + 1 no votes reject it, the draw goes on at n - f reveals; market.proof's theorems restated for n; an e2e scenario with 7 agents. Needed now that the Mac mini's agents have joined, 8 agents (market.soma settle and maybe_draw, market.proof, test/e2e.sh; several open tasks already: max_open_tasks, the leader's call)
M-1: the leader's pick sees each bid measured, as AlphaEvolve's evaluators measured programs: the shell applies each candidate and reports beside CI's result the OCaml lines it moves out of shell.ml (or the measure the task names), Any before and after, theorems and Examples added, a complexity measure (branches, longest function), and the time of a short e2e scenario where the deployment runs it; the pick prompt shows them as a table and the leader weighs them with its reading. Floor: the measures as a pure Soma function over a tree's files, checked by Examples; past it, recorded with the merge on chain (agent.soma pick_prompt, shell.ml, deploy/ci-command.py)
V-1: verified Soma, the other direction: code already in Soma gets precise types and proofs, a module or a property per bid: Any replaced by records and inductives where the code allows (node.soma's handle and effects, the market's obj fields), theorems for what the code promises (order_effects keeps every output once and decided ones before the snapshot). Floor: no new Any, existing proofs pass; measured by Any's count and the theorems added (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Two directions")
D-6: a node that can't reach a peer says so: log the peer and the reason (unresolvable, refused, timed out) when it first fails, when the reason changes and when it connects, not every second, and status.txt lists the peers with no link and why; a test whose genesis names a peer that doesn't resolve (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-6; shell.ml's dial loop in feed and resolve, or wherever step 2 put them)
U-1: the unikernel, first step: the node's I/O as one Soma interface, a datatype of its operations like Soma's Sandbox.io (peer links, timers and a millisecond clock, files, git, model and CI requests), which shell.ml interprets; Sandbox can then interpret the same program. Floor: the binary runs over it unchanged (e2e passes, same speed), and docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md lists what Sandbox still lacks for it (docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md "Two directions, and the unikernel"; shell.ml, node.soma)
P-1: the host's diplomats run with approvals off as the user that owns every node key, the inbox tokens and the push key: nodes, mirror and inbox server as a node user with 0700 homes, each diplomat its own user sharing only its inbox. Floor: host.sh does it, checked by a new test/ on a dry run that writes what root would (no root in CI); split it if too big (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-1 "The fix's design"; scripts/host.sh, scripts/bidder-inbox.sh, deploy/host/ci-sandbox, shell.ml repo_dir, docs/HOST.md, docs/SAFETY.md)
P-13: bound a node's memory against unauthenticated and Byzantine peers: authenticate before buffering, cap buffered bytes per connection and overall, votes carry no value, cap proposals per round, cap the message log by bytes (docs/SECURITY-REVIEW-2.md P-13; shell.ml's connection reader, consensus.soma)
D-2: model-call time limits and consensus timers use a monotonic clock, so a sleep or a clock step doesn't kill calls or fire every timer; a test that moves the wall clock under a running job (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-2; shell.ml start_job and timers)
D-3: a call the node kills takes its children with it: a process group per job, and bidder.sh ends its model on SIGTERM; a test that a killed job leaves nothing behind (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-3; shell.ml start_job, scripts/bidder.sh)
D-4: Grok Build's patches: GROK_PATCH_FLAGS with a lower default reasoning effort, then what its rejected bids share (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-4; scripts/bidder.sh)
D-7: a merge's votes can be read: an attestor's no carries its model's reason (attest BID no REASON) into the vote and the rejection, and status shows a pending merge's votes, reasons and the height it settles at; a test of a no with a reason through to the rejection and the status (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-7; agent.soma's attest reply, market.soma's attest rule and status_lines)
D-8: no calls on a finished task: the leader skips markq when the item's line at the head is already marked, and a node withdraws a patch request whose task closed (an inbox request leaves the inbox, so a one-at-a-time session isn't held up) and drops a patch reply for a task no longer open; a test for each (docs/DOGFOOD-REPORT.md D-8; agent.soma markq and the patch reply)
bonus: an agent runs its node as a binary (the extracted OCaml program) or as a MirageOS unikernel (Require Import Sandbox), from one source, and both kinds agree on every block; the unikernel is the end form: the node all Soma but Sandbox's bindings, verifiable and isolated, with a test of a unikernel node in a network of binaries (Soma's GRADUAL.md, Sandbox; docs/SHELL-IN-SOMA.md)
